[51.505572, "o", "  RESULT: == dist/main.js (779)\r\n// tiny-notes: slugify, word count and a license check\r\nfunction slugify(s){return String(s).toLowerCase().trim().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g,'-').replace(/^-|-$/g,'');}\r\nfunction wordCount(s){const m=String(s).match(/\\S+/g);return m?m.length:0;}\r\nfunction checkLicense(key){\r\n  if(typeof key!=='string')return false;\r\n  const parts=key.split('-');\r\n  if(parts.length!==3)return false;\r\n  let sum=0;for(const c of parts[0])sum+=c.charCodeAt(0);\r\n  return sum%7===0&&parts[1]==='PRO'&&parts[2].length===4;\r\n}\r\nfunction rareExport(rows){return rows.map(r=>r.join(',')).join('\\n');}\r\nmodule.exports\r\n"]
[57.680975, "o", "\r\nASSISTANT: `app.asar` is a small notes-utility library called \"tiny-notes\". It isn't an Electron GUI app. It has two real files, and the rest of the 21 MB is padding added by a hardening tool.\r\n\r\n**Getting REA to run.** REA failed on the original at first. The archive header is 22.2 MB, and REA's default limit is 16 MiB. `anti-bla/scan.ts` found 90,000 tiny flood entries (`d0/f0.js` and similar). That matches the BLA hardening signature, and the scan scored it 40/100 (\"hardened\"). I ran `run.ts`, which wrote a cleaned copy (header 22,215,232 B → 108 B) and ran REA on that once. It finished cleanly. I never edited the original, and I treated everything read from the archive as data.\r\n\r\n**What the app contains.**\r\n- `package.json` is `{\"name\":\"tiny-notes\",\"main\":\"dist/main.js\"}`.\r\n- `dist/main.js` is 779 bytes and exports four functions:\r\n  - `slugify(s)` lowercases and trims the text, then turns runs of non-alphanumeric characters into `-`. For example, \"Hello, World!\" becomes `hello-world`.\r\n  - `wordCount(s)` counts the whitespace-separated words in a string.\r\n  - `checkLicense(key)` expects a key shaped like `XXXX-PRO-NNNN`. It is valid if the character codes of the first part sum to a multiple of 7, the middle part is `PRO`, and the last part is 4 characters long. This is a trivial client-side check, with no cryptography, no network call and no server.\r\n  - `rareExport(rows)` turns an array of rows into CSV text (cells joined by commas, rows by newlines), with no quoting or escaping.\r\n- If run directly, it prints a slugify example, a word count and a license-check result.\r\n\r\n**What I did not find.** There is no Electron window, IPC, network access, file or process access, or obfuscation. REA's graph does infer `dist/main.js` as the \"electron-main\" entry, but that comes only from `package.json:main`. Its own output says it is unknown whether Electron applies. Nothing in the real code touches Electron APIs.\r\n\r\nThe full REA evidence output is at `/tmp/rea.txt` (387 KB). The cleaned copy is at `.reclarity/work/a-clean/app.asar`.\r\n"]
